Wait, let's check the logic carefully. This is exactly the kind of content issue I must evaluate independently.

Definitions:
- Parent (single-q tail predicate): gamma is visible from I+ iff ∃q ∈ I+, ∃t0 ∈ [0,T): gamma([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ∩ M.
- SET variant: gamma is contained in the union of J^-(q) over all q ∈ I+ (intersected with M).

Which is stronger? "Visible" as a property. Under parent (single-q), visibility = ∃q, tail ⊆ J^-(q). Under SET, visibility = γ([0,T)) ⊆ ∪_q J^-(q).

If γ ⊆ ∪ J^-(q), does that imply ∃ single q with a tail in J^-(q)? Not necessarily in general (the union may cover γ using different q's for different pieces). Conversely, if ∃q and t0 with γ([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ⊆ ∪ J^-(q), does that imply γ([0,T)) ⊆ ∪J^-(q)? Only if the initial segment γ([0,t0)) is also in the union. Hmm. Not necessarily, but for causal geodesics with endpoint... Actually in the union definition, typically the SET definition requires the entire geodesic contained in ∪_q J^-(q), i.e., including the initial segment. So neither obviously implies the other in full generality.

